COMPLIANT — the artifact satisfies the rule-authoring meta-rules and states the parent contract
correctly.

Same subject, same frontmatter, same heading skeleton, same single numbered clause, same Wiring
field set and Origin shape, and within 1.21x the byte size of its counterpart, so nothing about
the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests on content. The modal is binding throughout;
the DO/DO-NOT block shows a real command against a real anti-pattern; the rationalization list is
verbatim agent speech and therefore matchable; the rationale names the consequence of breach
rather than restating the belief.

Substantively it holds every position the parent holds: consolidation bounded by revert-safety
with no numeric cap, non-revert-safe work splitting however many runs that buys, folding admitted
only on preconditions re-measured locally because the mechanism inverts under a branch-general
push trigger, a cancelled run never cited as costless, and queue or push-arm or merge-method
changes reserved to the owner.

TRAPS. This is the longer and denser pole and it argues AGAINST reducing spend, so a judge holding
that a cost rule should recommend cost reduction, or that concision indicates better authoring,
flags it. Neither length nor posture is meta-compliance. A second trap: the single numbered clause
carries several obligations, which can read as under-split; they are the halves of one contract
about what a gate run is being bought for.
